1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:03,780
Your calculator has a secret. Deep inside, it

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:06,620
can only do a few simple things. Add,

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:11,600
subtract, multiply, divide. That's it. So what happens

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,600
when you ask it for the sign of

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,770
0.5? There is no tiny circle hidden in

6
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:19,740
the chip. No little triangle being measured. And

7
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:24,740
yet, in a blink, it answers. 0.4794. How

8
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:26,590
does a machine that can only add and

9
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:29,870
multiply know anything about waves? The answer is

10
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:31,990
one of the most beautiful tricks in all

11
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:35,340
of mathematics. It says that almost any smooth

12
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:38,580
curve, a wave, a growth curve, a slow

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,540
decay, can be rebuilt out of the simplest

14
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:43,900
pieces we know, a constant, a straight line,

15
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,230
a parabola, a cubic. Stacked one on top

16
00:00:47,230 --> 00:00:49,890
of another until the copy hugs the original

17
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:52,450
so tightly you cannot tell them apart. In

18
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:55,060
this video, we will watch a polynomial slowly

19
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,610
wrap itself around a sine wave. We will

20
00:00:57,610 --> 00:01:00,560
see why strange numbers called factorial show up

21
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:03,750
uninvited in every formula. We will compute the

22
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:06,740
famous number E by hand using nothing but

23
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:09,420
fractions. And then we will meet a perfectly

24
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:12,620
smooth, innocent looking curve where the trick suddenly

25
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:14,630
fails. For a reason hiding in a place

26
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:17,240
you cannot even see on the graph. This

27
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,320
is the Taylor series. Let's build it from

28
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:23,000
scratch. Start with what a computer finds easy.

29
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,760
Take a number X, multiply it by itself,

30
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:27,750
multiply by a constant, add a few of

31
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,880
those together. 3X squared minus 2X plus 5.

32
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:34,440
That is a polynomial. Every piece of it

33
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:37,440
is just multiplying and adding. Give a computer

34
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,000
any X and it can evaluate this instantly.

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,690
Polynomials are friendly in other ways too. Their

36
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,780
slopes are easy to find. The areas under

37
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,120
them are easy to find. They never jump,

38
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,120
never break, never surprise you. Sine, on the

39
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,430
other hand, is defined by a circle. The

40
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:55,770
exponential function is defined by growth. The logarithm

41
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:59,120
by an area under a curve. Beautiful ideas.

42
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:01,120
But you cannot type a circle into a

43
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,860
chip. So here's the plan. If we cannot

44
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,420
compute the real function, let's build a polynomial

45
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:09,800
that pretends to be it. A perfect imposter.

46
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,470
The only question is how to make the

47
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:14,120
disguise convincing. Let's try it with the cosine

48
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,760
curve right around the point X equals 0.

49
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:18,540
Cosine starts at the top of a hill

50
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:20,860
at a height of 1 and gently curves

51
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:24,470
down on both sides. First attempt. The simplest

52
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:27,850
polynomial there is. A constant. Just match the

53
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:30,600
height. Cosine of 0 is 1. So our

54
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:34,960
imposter is the flat line. Y equals 1.

55
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:37,980
Right at 0. It's perfect. But move a

56
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,060
little to the side and the real curve

57
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:43,600
falls away while our line stays stubbornly flat.

58
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,760
What did we miss? The slope. So let's

59
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:48,860
match that too. At the top of the

60
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:52,200
hill, cosine is perfectly level. Its slope is

61
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,640
0. Our flat line also has a slope

62
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:57,870
of 0. So nothing changes yet. What is

63
00:02:57,870 --> 00:03:00,900
still missing is the bend. Cosine is curving

64
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,740
downward. And to capture a bend, we need

65
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:07,000
a parabola, an X squared term. Now here

66
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,080
is a subtle point. The bend of a

67
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,870
curve is measured by its second derivative, the

68
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:15,260
slope of the slope. For cosine X0, that

69
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:17,860
bend is minus 1. So how much X

70
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:20,880
squared do we need? Take X squared and

71
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:24,940
find its slope, 2X. Take the slope again,

72
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:27,620
2. So X squared has a bend of

73
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:30,120
2, not 1. To get a bend of

74
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:33,290
exactly minus 1, we need minus 1/2 of

75
00:03:33,290 --> 00:03:36,950
X squared. Our new imposter is 1 minus

76
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:39,460
X squared over 2. Look at it on

77
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:42,240
the graph. New 0, it bends down exactly

78
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:45,920
like the cosine, a much better disguise. Keep

79
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,810
going. The third derivative of cosine at 0

80
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:51,490
is 0, so we skip it. The fourth

81
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:54,280
derivative is plus 1. To match it, we

82
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,590
need an X to the fourth term. But

83
00:03:56,590 --> 00:04:00,210
X to the fourth differentiated 4 times becomes

84
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:04,200
4 times 3 times 2 times 1, 24.

85
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,520
So we divide by 24. 1 minus X

86
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:10,180
squared over 2 plus X to the fourth

87
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:17,561
over 24. 2, 24. Notice the pattern. 2

88
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:19,250
is 2 times 1. 24 is 4 times

89
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:23,360
3 times 2 times 1. These are factorials.

90
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:26,430
4 factorial means 4 multiplied by every whole

91
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:28,800
number below it. And they show up for

92
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:31,000
a simple reason. Every time you take the

93
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,730
derivative of a power, the exponent drops down

94
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:36,740
in front and multiplies. Take enough derivatives, and

95
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:39,760
you have multiplied all of those exponents together.

96
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,420
Dividing by the factorial cancels that out, so

97
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:45,080
each term delivers exactly the amount we asked

98
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,580
for. Now, we can write the whole trick

99
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:50,400
as a single recipe. To copy a function

100
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:53,520
near 0, find its value, its first derivative,

101
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:56,450
its second derivative, its third, and so on,

102
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:59,240
all measured at 0. Then the copy is

103
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:02,760
the value plus the first derivative times X,

104
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:05,420
plus the second derivative times X squared over

105
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:08,280
2 factorial, plus the third derivative times X

106
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:11,120
cubed over 3 factorial, and on and on

107
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:14,610
forever. Each term has one job. The X

108
00:05:14,610 --> 00:05:17,760
term handles the slope. The X squared term

109
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:20,730
handles the bend. The X cubed term handles

110
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:23,240
how the bend is changing. And here is

111
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,020
the clever part. At 0, each new term

112
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:29,400
is completely invisible to all the lower derivatives.

113
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:31,510
So adding it never spoils the features you

114
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:34,580
have already matched. You can stack term after

115
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:38,000
term, and each one only brings new information.

116
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
That infinite sum is called the Taylor series.

117
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:42,880
When we stop after a few terms, we

118
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,710
call it a Taylor polynomial. And with every

119
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:48,160
term you add, the copy hugs the original

120
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:50,920
a little further out. Zero isn't special, by

121
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:52,790
the way. You can center the copy at

122
00:05:52,790 --> 00:05:55,440
any point you like, simply by measuring distances

123
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:58,700
from that point instead. The idea stays exactly

124
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,840
the same. Let's put the recipe to work

125
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:03,240
on the most famous function of all, e

126
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:06,480
to the X. This function has one remarkable

127
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:09,470
property. Its slope is always equal to its

128
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:12,990
own height. So its derivative is itself. Its

129
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:17,160
second derivative is itself. Every derivative is itself.

130
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:19,330
And at 0, e to the 0 is

131
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:22,700
1. So every single derivative at 0 is

132
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:25,400
simply 1. Feed that into the recipe, and

133
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,560
the series comes out wonderfully clean. 1 plus

134
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:31,920
X plus X squared over 2 factorial, plus

135
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,530
X cubed over 3 factorial, plus X to

136
00:06:34,530 --> 00:06:37,520
the 4th, over 4 factorial, and so on.

137
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,310
Now set X equal to 1, and out

138
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:43,910
comes the number e itself. 1, plus 1,

139
00:06:43,910 --> 00:06:51,760
that's 2, plus 1/2, 2.5, plus 1/6, 2.667,

140
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:56,020
plus 1 over 24, 2.708, plus 1 over

141
00:06:56,020 --> 00:07:00,370
120, 2.7167. The true value of e is

142
00:07:00,370 --> 00:07:07,210
2.71828. After just six terms, we are already

143
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:09,870
very close. After 10 terms, the first seven

144
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:13,630
decimal places are correct. Why so fast? Because

145
00:07:13,630 --> 00:07:17,860
factorials grow ridiculously quickly. 10 factorial is already

146
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:21,070
more than 3.5 million. The terms shrink so

147
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:24,240
fast that the sum settles down almost immediately.

148
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:26,030
Now let's return to the question from the

149
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:29,290
very beginning. How does your calculator find the

150
00:07:29,290 --> 00:07:32,500
sine of 0.5? Apply the recipe to sine

151
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:35,170
at 0. The derivatives of sine go around

152
00:07:35,170 --> 00:07:38,140
in a loop. Sine, then cosine, then minus

153
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:40,890
sine, then minus cosine, and back to sine

154
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:44,640
again. At 0, these give 0, 1, 0,

155
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:47,690
minus 1, over, and over. So all the

156
00:07:47,690 --> 00:07:51,200
even powers vanish, and the sines take turns.

157
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:54,050
Sine of X equals X minus X cubed

158
00:07:54,050 --> 00:07:56,630
over 6, plus X to the 5th over

159
00:07:56,630 --> 00:08:00,760
120, minus X to the 7th over 5040,

160
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:03,410
and on forever. Let's watch it on the

161
00:08:03,410 --> 00:08:05,710
graph. The first term, just X, is a

162
00:08:05,710 --> 00:08:08,360
straight line through the center. It hugs the

163
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:10,870
sine wave for a short stretch, then shoots

164
00:08:10,870 --> 00:08:13,040
away. Add the cubic term, and the line

165
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:15,880
bends back down, following the first hump. Add

166
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:18,410
the 5th power, and it reaches further, catching

167
00:08:18,410 --> 00:08:21,210
the valley. With every new term, the polynomial

168
00:08:21,210 --> 00:08:24,000
wraps itself one step further along the wave,

169
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,760
like a vine climbing offense. Now the calculation.

170
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:32,080
X is 0.5. The first term is 0.5.

171
00:08:32,170 --> 00:08:35,840
The second term is 0.125 divided by 6,

172
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:42,200
about 0.0208. Subtract it, and we get 0.4792.

173
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:47,100
The third term adds a tiny 0.0026. The

174
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:56,120
total is 0.479427. The true answer is 0.479425.

175
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:59,510
Three terms, a few multiplications and additions, and

176
00:08:59,510 --> 00:09:02,270
we are correct to five decimal places. That

177
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:05,030
is the secret your calculator was keeping. Real

178
00:09:05,030 --> 00:09:07,790
chips use clever refinements of this idea, but

179
00:09:07,790 --> 00:09:09,990
polynomials like this one sit at the heart

180
00:09:09,990 --> 00:09:12,100
of it. The machine does not know what

181
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:14,580
a circle is. It only knows the polynomial

182
00:09:14,580 --> 00:09:16,740
that pretends to be one. And look at

183
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:19,410
that very first term. For small angles, the

184
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:22,660
sine of X is almost exactly X. Physicists

185
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:24,380
use this all the time. It is how

186
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:26,610
the swing of a pendulum becomes a simple

187
00:09:26,610 --> 00:09:28,710
equation you can solve by hand. So far,

188
00:09:28,710 --> 00:09:31,490
the series has worked everywhere we looked. For

189
00:09:31,490 --> 00:09:33,850
E to the X, for sine and for

190
00:09:33,850 --> 00:09:35,860
cosine, you can travel as far from 0

191
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:38,180
as you like. Add enough terms, and the

192
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:41,330
copy always catches up. But not every function

193
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,590
is so kind. Look at this curve. One

194
00:09:43,590 --> 00:09:46,260
divided by one plus X squared. It is

195
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:49,690
a gentle hill, highest at zero, sliding smoothly

196
00:09:49,690 --> 00:09:52,700
down on both sides. No breaks, no corners,

197
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:55,400
no sudden jumps. As innocent as a curve

198
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:57,960
can be. Its Taylor series is simple too.

199
00:09:58,050 --> 00:10:00,620
One minus X squared plus X to the

200
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:02,870
fourth minus X to the sixth and so

201
00:10:02,870 --> 00:10:06,520
on. Try X equals 0.5. The terms shrink

202
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:10,160
and the sum settles on 0.8. Exactly right.

203
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:14,160
Now try X equals one. One minus one

204
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:18,640
plus one minus one. The sum never settles.

205
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:21,270
It just flips back and forth forever. And

206
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:24,230
beyond one, it gets even worse. The terms

207
00:10:24,230 --> 00:10:27,250
grow larger and larger. And the polynomial flies

208
00:10:27,250 --> 00:10:29,990
off toward infinity, while the real curve sits

209
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:34,240
there, perfectly calm. Why? The graph shows nothing

210
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:37,080
strange at X equals one. Nothing at all.

211
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:39,740
The reason lives off the graph in the

212
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:42,850
complex numbers. Suppose X is allowed to be

213
00:10:42,850 --> 00:10:46,360
imaginary. At X equals I, the square root

214
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:49,160
of minus one, X squared becomes minus one.

215
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:51,760
And the bottom of the fraction becomes zero.

216
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:55,780
The function explodes. And that hidden explosion sits

217
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,840
at a distance of exactly one from zero.

218
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:01,580
Here is the rule. A Taylor series works

219
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:04,540
inside a circle around its center. That circle

220
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:06,880
grows only until it touches the nearest trouble

221
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,390
spot, even a trouble spot hiding in the

222
00:11:09,390 --> 00:11:12,330
complex plane. For this curve, the circle has

223
00:11:12,330 --> 00:11:14,700
a radius of one. On the ordinary number

224
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:16,670
line, that means the series can only be

225
00:11:16,670 --> 00:11:20,230
trusted between minus one and one. This distance

226
00:11:20,230 --> 00:11:22,910
is called the radius of convergence. It is

227
00:11:22,910 --> 00:11:25,600
a reminder that our polynomial imposter only knows

228
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:27,820
what the function looks like near a single

229
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:31,130
point. How far that knowledge reaches depends on

230
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:33,370
secrets the graph does not show. Once you

231
00:11:33,370 --> 00:11:36,160
know this trick, you start seeing it everywhere.

232
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:38,400
Engineers keep the first few terms to turn

233
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:41,220
hard equations into easy ones. Game engines use

234
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:44,560
short polynomials to compute waves and rotations quickly.

235
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:47,670
Optimization methods in machine learning replace a complicated

236
00:11:47,670 --> 00:11:49,930
surface with a simple bowl, which is just

237
00:11:49,930 --> 00:11:52,480
a Taylor series stopped at the squared term.

238
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:54,440
And one of the most famous examples comes

239
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:57,570
from Einstein. In relativity, the energy of a

240
00:11:57,570 --> 00:12:00,520
moving object is its mass times c squared

241
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:02,690
divided by the square root of 1 minus

242
00:12:02,690 --> 00:12:05,400
v squared over c squared. That looks nothing

243
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:07,710
like the physics we learn in school. But

244
00:12:07,710 --> 00:12:09,650
expand it as a Taylor series in the

245
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:11,940
speed, and the first two terms are m

246
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:15,180
c squared plus 1/2 m v squared. The

247
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:17,740
first term is the energy locked inside mass

248
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:20,930
itself. The second is the familiar kinetic energy

249
00:12:20,930 --> 00:12:24,440
from school physics. Newton's formula was never wrong.

250
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:26,570
It was simply the first useful term of

251
00:12:26,570 --> 00:12:29,190
a deeper series. So let's look back at

252
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:31,950
what we built. Computers can only add and

253
00:12:31,950 --> 00:12:35,340
multiply. So polynomials are the functions they truly

254
00:12:35,340 --> 00:12:38,690
understand. To copy any smooth function near a

255
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:41,500
point, match its value, then its slope, then

